217
00:23:25,380 --> 00:23:28,730
so my name is Matt Walter from TT IC and I'm very happy to share this

218
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:37,670
nest next sponsorship session So our first speaker is Sean Harris joining us from GM Hi,

219
00:23:37,720 --> 00:23:38,510
my name's Sean.

220
00:23:38,560 --> 00:23:42,220
I'm here to talk about innovations in autonomy at GM.

221
00:23:43,480 --> 00:23:44,690
So first quick intro to me.

222
00:23:44,740 --> 00:23:45,620
I'm Sean.

223
00:23:45,780 --> 00:23:47,420
I lead autonomy at GM.

224
00:23:47,980 --> 00:23:50,980
I've been in the autonomous vehicle space for over 10 years now.

225
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:55,020
First at Cruise and then at Wave and now at General Motors.

226
00:23:55,960 --> 00:23:57,710
And three topics I'd like to go through today.

227
00:23:57,760 --> 00:24:01,420
The first is GM's vision for autonomy.

228
00:24:02,340 --> 00:24:09,240
The second is some different innovations and approaches on how to learn to drive both imitation learning and reinforcement learning.

229
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:14,920
And then also talk about how VLAs are changing the way we think about autonomy as well.

230
00:24:16,400 --> 00:24:19,240
So first up, GM and autonomy.

231
00:24:19,960 --> 00:24:23,280
You might not have heard of a lot of GM autonomy previously.

232
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:29,170
The GM autonomy team is formed from the cruise robot taxi team and the GM supercruise team.

233
00:24:29,220 --> 00:24:35,860
So early 2025, GM closed down the cruise robot taxi program and absorbed that team into GM.

234
00:24:36,200 --> 00:24:38,820
And now we are focused on building personal autonomy.

235
00:24:39,180 --> 00:24:43,920
And the goal here is to give both safety and time back to drivers.

236
00:24:44,440 --> 00:24:48,630
And personal autonomy here is really focused on the car that's in your driveway,

237
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:53,400
like your personal car and how it can be safer and how it can give you more time back.

238
00:24:54,720 --> 00:25:02,500
Our next product goal is focused on an eyes off highway product in 2028 and then scaling to urban and other ODDs after that.

239
00:25:03,020 --> 00:25:08,250
Eyes off product here means that when you get to the highway, you press go, and then the car does all the work.

240
00:25:08,300 --> 00:25:10,770
You don't have to do any driving yourself.

241
00:25:10,820 --> 00:25:12,070
You don't have to monitor the system.

242
00:25:12,120 --> 00:25:16,180
You can read your emails, browse Reddit, check your phone, whatever you need to do.

243
00:25:16,560 --> 00:25:21,960
So it's a high bar of performance that we're aiming to achieve that genuinely gives time back.

244
00:25:23,690 --> 00:25:27,380
Okay, so let's get into some of the technology we've been experimenting with.

245
00:25:27,430 --> 00:25:29,960
So first is around self -play.

246
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:38,820
Self -play became popular in AI probably in the 2017, 2018 kind of time, with AlphaGo.

247
00:25:39,990 --> 00:25:48,290
And the idea of self -play is that you have an agent play against itself in simulation to learn a robust policy for how to solve a problem.

248
00:25:48,600 --> 00:25:58,960
And in the space of Go or chess, you're able to play a lot of games in simulation and get to a far superior policy than what a human could do.

249
00:25:59,120 --> 00:26:02,040
And that's why it's very hard to beat a computer at chess or Go these days.

250
00:26:02,700 --> 00:26:04,290
And in 2025,

251
00:26:04,340 --> 00:26:08,950
there was a paper on general autonomy emerges from self -play that kind of showed

252
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:13,300
that self -play can also be a thing we do in the autonomy space as well.

253
00:26:15,040 --> 00:26:23,250
And so at GM, we built a simulation environment that can run at 50,000 times or more than 50,000 times real -time,

254
00:26:23,300 --> 00:26:30,390
and that enables us to do a really large volume of reinforcement learning and enables this kind of self -play to happen.

255
00:26:30,440 --> 00:26:37,960
So here's some examples in, you know, sort of 15 minutes, you don't really learn much, but some very basic behavior comes out.

256
00:26:38,380 --> 00:26:43,730
In an hour of training, you start to get, you know, reasonable policies and agents start to move around.

257
00:26:43,780 --> 00:26:46,290
And after, you know, in sort of less than two hours of training,

258
00:26:46,340 --> 00:26:55,680
you can have pretty robust policies emerge with very human behaviors in terms of merging, pushing in, giving way, taking turns.

259
00:26:56,680 --> 00:27:00,500
And this is not a super complicated environment, but it is a very large multimodal space.

260
00:27:01,220 --> 00:27:04,660
So being able to play against yourself in simulation can be a really powerful tool.

261
00:27:06,030 --> 00:27:09,380
So some advantages that self -play can give us.

262
00:27:09,430 --> 00:27:11,810
The first is on scale and coverage.

263
00:27:12,590 --> 00:27:18,240
So because this is done in simulation, we can go and create really rare or adversarial events quite easily.

264
00:27:18,290 --> 00:27:23,600
We don't have to wait to see them on the road, and that gives us a really broad set of training examples that we can use,

265
00:27:23,650 --> 00:27:26,990
and it makes getting coverage over the problem space much easier.

266
00:27:28,500 --> 00:27:37,720
We can also surpass the ceiling of human performance the same way that AlphaGo got much better than humans at playing Go,

267
00:27:37,770 --> 00:27:40,630
we can also get better than humans at driving cars.

268
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:49,050
And also you learn to recover, so you sort of avoid this dagger problem where you get into a state that you don't know how to get out of,

269
00:27:49,100 --> 00:27:52,150
because in simulation you can explore the full state space.

270
00:27:52,200 --> 00:27:58,690
Some challenges of self play though, the first is that it doesn't necessarily match the real world distribution,

271
00:27:58,740 --> 00:28:04,660
which means that you might spend time optimizing for situations that are not very common on the road.

272
00:28:05,300 --> 00:28:13,430
You also don't have any guarantees that the trajectory you get is a natural human like one, and people want the products to feel human at the end.

273
00:28:13,480 --> 00:28:16,140
So that might not necessarily be what people want.

274
00:28:17,360 --> 00:28:21,980
And the last one is that you can only take a simplified input of the world.

275
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:28,490
Because we want to train in such high volumes, much faster than real time, we're not using raw sensor inputs here.

276
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:32,260
We're using sort of perception inputs like boxes and lane lines and those kinds of things.

277
00:28:32,500 --> 00:28:35,500
So it limits the amount of information you can get to the model.

278
00:28:37,250 --> 00:28:38,900
And this is not just research at GM.

279
00:28:38,950 --> 00:28:40,800
So this is part of our production release.

280
00:28:40,850 --> 00:28:45,770
This is a tool that we use for cars driving on the road today.

281
00:28:47,510 --> 00:28:50,410
Okay, another complementary technology here is imitation learning.

282
00:28:51,050 --> 00:28:56,730
Imitation learning is imitating humans and learning to imitate human driving.

283
00:28:58,060 --> 00:29:02,780
The idea here is that you have a large corpus of human driving logs.

284
00:29:03,080 --> 00:29:08,270
You use them as supervision until the model, hey, copy this human driving and that becomes your policy.

285
00:29:08,320 --> 00:29:12,800
So the strengths of this is that matching the real world distribution is quite

286
00:29:12,850 --> 00:29:17,050
easy because you already have data from the real world that that matches that distribution.

287
00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:24,120
Getting human like behavior is also very straightforward because you're already copying the human by design.

288
00:29:24,740 --> 00:29:29,540
And you can also take in raw sensor input enables you to do sort of full full end to end learning.

289
00:29:30,400 --> 00:29:35,920
The challenge here, though, is that you now have to actually go and collect real world data.

290
00:29:36,140 --> 00:29:42,700
which means that if an event happens once every million miles, you don't have to drive a million miles just to get one training example.

291
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:46,510
You also now have a ceiling of human performance.

292
00:29:46,560 --> 00:29:49,240
It's hard to get better than human when you're learning from humans.

293
00:29:49,950 --> 00:29:55,730
And you don't solve the dagger problem, where you can get yourself in a state you don't know how to get out of necessarily.

294
00:29:56,740 --> 00:29:58,810
And at GM, we have a really big corpus.

295
00:29:58,860 --> 00:30:05,540
We have hundreds of thousands of hours of training data that we can use to help learn robust policies here.

296
00:30:06,710 --> 00:30:10,920
And so in theory, we have two very complimentary techniques here,

297
00:30:10,970 --> 00:30:15,450
so we should be able to bring them together into something that includes the strengths of both.

298
00:30:16,110 --> 00:30:21,780
Now, I won't share exactly how we do this at GM, but here's a couple of examples of ways we could do this,

299
00:30:21,830 --> 00:30:25,680
and I'll leave this as a thought exercise to think about afterwards.

300
00:30:25,730 --> 00:30:28,860
One idea is you could use the human policy as an anchor.

301
00:30:28,910 --> 00:30:32,270
So you start with an imitation learn model, and then you say, okay,

302
00:30:32,570 --> 00:30:36,780
you bound the reinforcement learning update and sort of keep it within this global maximum.

303
00:30:36,830 --> 00:30:39,230
Say, hey, okay, you can't vary too much from the human policy.

304
00:30:40,060 --> 00:30:48,460
Another would be to learn a teacher model in self -play in the RL environment and then use that, sort of distill that down into something else.

305
00:30:49,100 --> 00:30:55,200
And then you could also learn rewards from human driving and use those in your reinforcement learning self -play environment.

306
00:30:57,220 --> 00:30:59,910
Okay, last thing I wanted to touch on then is VLAs,

307
00:30:59,960 --> 00:31:04,840
they've changed a lot of how work gets done in many industries and the same is true in self -driving.

308
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:09,520
So we'll talk about one on the car application and many off -board applications.

309
00:31:09,680 --> 00:31:13,410
So on car application, really obvious one is sort of scene understanding,

310
00:31:13,460 --> 00:31:17,360
being able to reason about a scene and then decide at a high level what to do.

311
00:31:18,330 --> 00:31:18,920
So on the right,

312
00:31:18,970 --> 00:31:25,340
we have a relatively straightforward one of a traffic stop where there's a police car on the side of the road and a model here is able to say,

313
00:31:25,390 --> 00:31:26,820
hey, there's a police car, don't drive there.

314
00:31:26,870 --> 00:31:28,480
And there's also a caution zone next to it.

315
00:31:28,530 --> 00:31:30,310
Say, hey, you prefer not to drive here.

316
00:31:30,730 --> 00:31:35,230
And then a high level action can be change lanes, one lane to the left.

317
00:31:36,320 --> 00:31:39,980
And then on the left side, we have a more complicated scenario where there's a crash site, there's multiple.
